<article class="env-lemma" id="0H2V">
  <p><a class="environment-identifier" href="/tag/0H2V">Lemma <span data-tag="0H2V">53.22.5</span>.</a> In Situation <a href="/tag/0B5D" data-tag="0B5D">53.6.2</a>, assume $X$ is geometrically integral over $k$ and has genus $g$. Let $\mathcal{L}$ be an invertible $\mathcal{O}_ X$-module. If $\deg (\mathcal{L}) \geq 2g + 1$, then $\mathcal{L}$ is very ample. </p>
</article><article class="env-proof">
    <p>
      <strong>Proof.</strong>
      By Lemma <a href="/tag/0E3C" data-tag="0E3C">53.22.3</a>, $\mathcal{L}$ is globally generated, and so it determines a morphism $f : X \to \mathbf{P}^ n_ k$ where $n = h^0(X,\mathcal{L}) - 1$. To show that $\mathcal{L}$ is very ample means to show that $f$ is a closed immersion. It suffices to check that the base change of $f$ to an algebraic closure $\overline{k}$ of $k$ is a closed immersion (Descent, Lemma <a href="/tag/02L6" data-tag="02L6">35.23.21</a>). So we may assume that $k$ is algebraically closed; $X$ remains integral, by assumption. Lemma <a href="/tag/0E3D" data-tag="0E3D">53.22.4</a> gives that for every $0$-dimensional closed subscheme $Z\subset X$ of degree 2, the restriction map $H^0(X, \mathcal{L}) \to H^0(X, \mathcal{L}|_ Z)$ is surjective. By Varieties, Lemma <a href="/tag/0E8T" data-tag="0E8T">33.23.2</a>, $\mathcal{L}$ is very ample. 
      <span class="qed">$\square$</span>
    </p>
</article>